  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 1229 AM MDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nFairly good model agreement through Tuesday, then dramatic \ndifferences Wednesday onward. NBM seems to lean more towards what \nthe GFS is selling for the latter half of the week so will speak to \nthat solution, but realize that the forecast remains very fluid.\n\nMonday...Upper trough moves ever so slightly to the east through \nMonday, with the upper low center dropping south into NV after \nmidnight. Strangely, this will lead to a brief lull in shower and \nstorm activity along the Continental Divide through the morning, \nwith activity then expected to pick up through the evening. \nIncreased cloud cover for the western sections of the forecast area \nwill lead to a few degrees of cooling for the high valleys, with \nhighs in the mid 60s to near 70F, while the eastern plains remain \nwarm and dry with highs in the upper 70s to upper 80s.\n\nTuesday...Trough continues on the slow progression to the east, with \nthe upper low pushing into UT through the day. GFS indicated the low \ncentroid actually dives a little further south into southern UT, \ncloser to the actual Four Corners. Regardless, precipitation starts \nto increase in coverage once again across the higher terrain, and as \ncolder air filters in, precip across mt peaks will make the switch \nover to snow. Weak snow amounts persist, with maybe 2-3 inches for \nthe eastern San Juans, and up to 4 inches for the peaks in Lake \nCounty. Precip finally starts to spread east into the Pikes Peak \nregion Tue evening. Cold front previously promised has been pushed \noff to the following day, so plan on one more warm and basically dry \nday for the plains with highs in the 70s to lower 80s. The high \nvalleys will be cooler, with afternoon highs in the 60s.\n\nWednesday...Upper low ejects northeast across the NW quad of CO \nthrough the day, reaching South Dakota by early Thu morning. \nHowever, this solution is 6-12 hrs slower than previous runs, which \nmay be a hint of changes to come - we shall see. Precip finally \nspreads to the eastern plains, as does the cold front, so finally \nsome temperature relief arrives. Look for high temps in the 50s to \naround 60F for the high valleys, and 60s to around 70F for the \nplains.\n\nThursday through Saturday...Upper low continues moving out of the \nregion on Thu, allowing brisk N-NW flow to briefly settle in before \na ridge of high pressure builds in. This will lead to a couple of \ncool but dry days before warming returns by the weekend. Looking at \nthe EC solution, throw all of that out the window and insert a big \nquestion mark. Stay tuned. Moore",
